Q1. Partitioning

Code

  CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS prac;
  USE prac;

  CREATE TABLE student (
     roll_no INT,
     name    VARCHAR(30),
     dept    VARCHAR(10),
     marks   INT
  )
  PARTITION BY RANGE (marks) (
     PARTITION p0 VALUES LESS THAN (40),
     PARTITION p1 VALUES LESS THAN (70),
     PARTITION p2 VALUES LESS THAN MAXVALUE
  );

  INSERT INTO student VALUES
  (1,'Asha','IT',35),
  (2,'Ravi','CS',55),
  (3,'Neha','IT',82),
  (4,'Om','CS',68),
  (5,'Priya','IT',73),
  (6,'Amit','CS',48);

  UPDATE student SET marks = 45 WHERE roll_no = 1;
  UPDATE student SET dept = 'CS' WHERE roll_no = 3;

  DELETE FROM student WHERE roll_no = 4;

  SELECT * FROM student PARTITION (p0);
  SELECT * FROM student PARTITION (p1);
  SELECT * FROM student PARTITION (p2);

  SELECT PARTITION_NAME, PARTITION_METHOD, PARTITION_DESCRIPTION, TABLE_ROWS
  FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.PARTITIONS
  WHERE TABLE_SCHEMA = 'prac' AND TABLE_NAME = 'student';

Partition-wise data storage
 • Partitioning: one logical table is split into smaller physical parts called partitions, based on
   a partition key column (here marks).
 • Range partitioning: each partition holds rows within a range of the key. Marks below 40
   go to p0, 40 to 69 to p1, 70 and above to p2.

 • Hash partitioning: MySQL computes (key value MOD number of partitions) and the result
   decides the partition, so rows spread evenly.
 • Working: on INSERT, MySQL stores the row in the matching partition automatically; on
   UPDATE of the key, the row moves to the new partition. A query that filters on the key reads
   only the needed partition (partition pruning), so retrieval is faster.

Q2. Distributed Transactions

1. Distributed transaction
A distributed transaction is a single transaction that updates data stored in more than one
database or site. All the parts must succeed together or fail together. Example: Rahul (Bank1)
transfers Rs 1,000 to Amit (Bank2). The debit happens in Bank1 and the credit in Bank2, so
both databases take part in one transaction.

Full SQL example

  CREATE DATABASE Bank1;
  CREATE DATABASE Bank2;

  USE Bank1;
  CREATE TABLE Account (account_no INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(50), balance INT);
  INSERT INTO Account VALUES (101, 'Rahul', 5000);

  USE Bank2;
  CREATE TABLE Account (account_no INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(50), balance INT);
  INSERT INTO Account VALUES (102, 'Amit', 3000);

  START TRANSACTION;

  UPDATE Bank1.Account SET balance = balance - 1000 WHERE account_no = 101;

  UPDATE Bank2.Account SET balance = balance + 1000 WHERE account_no = 102;

  SELECT * FROM Bank1.Account;
  SELECT * FROM Bank2.Account;

  COMMIT;

  SELECT * FROM Bank1.Account;
  SELECT * FROM Bank2.Account;

2. Purpose of COMMIT and ROLLBACK
 • COMMIT: makes all changes of the transaction permanent in the database. Used when
   every step succeeded. After COMMIT the change cannot be undone.
 • ROLLBACK: cancels all changes made since the transaction began and restores the old
   values. Used when any step fails, so money is never debited without being credited.

3. Why transaction recovery is required
 • Failures happen at any time. A power cut, system crash, network failure or deadlock can
   stop a transaction in the middle.
 • Atomicity. A transaction must complete fully or not at all. If Rs 1,000 is debited from Rahul
   but the system crashes before crediting Amit, recovery undoes the debit.
 • Consistency. Without recovery the database is left with half-finished changes, such as
   money lost or a seat booked without payment.

 • Durability. Changes of committed transactions must survive a crash. Recovery redoes
   them from the log.
 • Reliability in distributed systems. If one site fails after the other has committed,
   recovery with two-phase commit makes all sites either commit or roll back, so the
   databases stay in agreement.

Q5. Star Schema and Snowflake Schema

Star schema
A star schema has one central fact table (Fact_Sales) joined directly to denormalized
dimension tables. Each dimension is a single flat table, so the diagram looks like a star. It
keeps data in a denormalized form (not normalized beyond 2NF), so city, state, category and
month sit inside one table.

